third_party/ — vendored dependencies
====================================

Everything here is third-party source compiled into bu. Rules:

  * Vendor upstream release files verbatim. Never edit them in place: trim
    and configure through compile-time defines in CMakeLists.txt, so a version
    bump is a file copy and a diff against upstream is empty.
  * Each library is its own CMake object library built with -w and its own
    flags (-O3; see CMakeLists.txt). Our warnings and no-STL rules apply to
    src/, not to vendored C.
  * Permissive licences only. Each library's licence file sits beside it,
    and THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES at the top of the tree collects the licence
    texts that ship with a binary. Update it when a library is added or its
    licence file changes.
  * Each header is included by exactly one bu translation unit (named
    below), so replacing a library is a one-file change.
  * Record the exact version, the URL of the release artifact and its sha256,
    so the next bump is reproducible and the provenance checkable.

Inventory
---------

BLAKE3  1.8.7  —  CC0-1.0 OR Apache-2.0 (OR Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception);
                   bu uses it under CC0-1.0
    Upstream: https://github.com/BLAKE3-team/BLAKE3/archive/refs/tags/1.8.7.tar.gz
    sha256:   c6782a28842b1c0478524ac06a4f2ede784038ee298d6e2162c0b089c4306a3c
    Files:    blake3/{blake3.c,blake3.h,blake3_impl.h,blake3_dispatch.c,
              blake3_portable.c,blake3_sse41.c,blake3_avx2.c,blake3_avx512.c}
              from c/, plus LICENSE_A2, LICENSE_A2LLVM, LICENSE_CC0.
    Target:   bk_blake3. Header confined to src/crypto/crypto.cpp.
    Config:   BLAKE3_NO_SSE2 — bu refuses CPUs without AES-NI, and every
              AES-NI CPU has SSE4.1, so the SSE2 variant could never be
              dispatched to. The intrinsics files are compiled with -msse4.1,
              -mavx2 and -mavx512f -mavx512vl respectively; blake3_dispatch.c
              picks one at run time from CPUID, as upstream intends. The
              assembly variants are not used (the intrinsics files are the
              upstream-recommended portable-build path and LTO can see them).
    Why:      the fastest well-reviewed hash with a keyed mode (chunk IDs are a
              MAC of the content) and a derive_key mode (subkeys) in one
              primitive; SIMD-parallel even within a 16 KB chunk.

LZ4  1.10.0  —  BSD-2-Clause (lib/ only)
    Upstream: https://github.com/lz4/lz4/releases/download/v1.10.0/lz4-1.10.0.tar.gz
    sha256:   537512904744b35e232912055ccf8ec66d768639ff3abe5788d90d792ec5f48b
    Files:    lz4/lz4.c, lz4/lz4.h, lz4/LICENSE (= lib/LICENSE).
    Target:   bk_lz4. Header confined to src/format/codec.cpp.
    Config:   none; the block API only (LZ4_compress_fast_extState,
              LZ4_decompress_safe). --gc-sections drops the streaming and
              dictionary entry points.
    Why:      compression that is never the bottleneck behind disk I/O
              (~700 MB/s per core compressing, several GB/s decompressing),
              with a bounds-checked decoder.

libsodium  1.0.22  —  ISC; the Argon2 files (argon2/) and the BLAKE2b
                         reference files (blake2b/) are used under CC0-1.0,
                         as their headers allow (CC0 text: blake3/LICENSE_CC0)
    Upstream: https://github.com/jedisct1/libsodium/releases/download/1.0.22-RELEASE/libsodium-1.0.22.tar.gz
    sha256:   adbdd8f16149e81ac6078a03aca6fc03b592b89ef7b5ed83841c086191be3349
    Files (paths after "=" are upstream's, under src/libsodium/; every file
    is byte-identical to the release tarball's):
      AES-256-GCM
              libsodium/aead_aes256gcm_aesni.c
                (= crypto_aead/aes256gcm/aesni/aead_aes256gcm_aesni.c)
      Argon2id
              libsodium/argon2/{argon2.c,argon2.h,argon2-core.c,argon2-core.h,
                argon2-encoding.c,argon2-encoding.h,argon2-fill-block-ref.c,
                argon2-fill-block-ssse3.c,argon2-fill-block-avx2.c,
                argon2-fill-block-avx512f.c,blake2b-long.c,blake2b-long.h,
                blamka-round-ref.h,blamka-round-ssse3.h,blamka-round-avx2.h,
                blamka-round-avx512f.h,pwhash_argon2id.c}
                (= crypto_pwhash/argon2/*)
              libsodium/blake2b/{blake2b-ref.c,blake2b-compress-ref.c,blake2.h,
                generichash_blake2b.c}
                (= crypto_generichash/blake2b/ref/*)
              libsodium/runtime.c (= sodium/runtime.c): CPU feature detection
                that picks the Argon2 kernel.
              libsodium/codecs.c (= sodium/codecs.c): base64, referenced only
                by argon2.c's encoded-string API, which bu never calls.
      Headers
              libsodium/include/sodium/{core.h,crypto_aead_aes256gcm.h,
                crypto_generichash_blake2b.h,crypto_pwhash_argon2id.h,
                crypto_verify_16.h,export.h,randombytes.h,runtime.h,utils.h,
                private/common.h,private/implementations.h,
                private/sse2_64_32.h}  (declarations the files above include)
              libsodium/LICENSE
              libsodium/shim/sodium_shim.c — NOT upstream: written for bu
                (MIT, like src/). Defines the support symbols the vendored
                files reference (sodium_misuse, sodium_memzero, sodium_memcmp,
                crypto_verify_16, randombytes_buf) so that these files can be
                vendored instead of the library and its init/RNG/allocator
                machinery.
    Targets:  bk_aesgcm (AES-GCM + shim), bk_argon2 (Argon2id, runtime.c,
              codecs.c), bk_blake2b. Headers confined to src/crypto/crypto.cpp.
    Config:   bk_aesgcm: CONFIGURED=1 NATIVE_LITTLE_ENDIAN=1 HAVE_TMMINTRIN_H=1
              HAVE_WMMINTRIN_H=1 HAVE_GCC_MEMORY_FENCES=1 — what libsodium's
              configure would define on x86-64 Linux.
              bk_argon2: the same base plus HAVE_{EMM,PMM,TMM,SMM,AVX,AVX2,
              AVX512F,WMM}INTRIN_H, HAVE_CPUID, HAVE_AVX_ASM (runtime.c's
              CPUID/XGETBV paths), HAVE_MMAP and HAVE_SYS_MMAN_H (the Argon2
              matrix is an anonymous mapping, returned to the kernel by munmap).
              The SSSE3/AVX2/AVX-512F fill-block files set their own target
              pragmas; argon2-core.c picks one at run time, after
              src/crypto/crypto.cpp has called _sodium_runtime_get_cpu_features
              (what sodium_init would do).
              bk_blake2b: no SIMD defines, so only the portable BLAKE2b
              compression is compiled and dispatched to. Argon2 uses BLAKE2b
              for its first and last blocks only; the per-pass work is the
              fill-block kernel.
    Why:      AES-256-GCM: a vetted, self-contained AES-256-GCM over AES-NI +
              PCLMULQDQ (7 blocks in parallel with aggregated GHASH) by the
              libsodium author, with runtime target attributes so it compiles
              into a generic x86-64 binary. Needs AES-NI, PCLMULQDQ and AVX at
              run time; src/crypto/crypto.cpp refuses other CPUs by name.
              There is no VAES/VPCLMULQDQ (AVX-512 wide) path in this release;
              see docs/design.md for what that would buy.
              Argon2id: the memory-hard passphrase KDF of RFC 9106 (and of
              LUKS2), for the key file's passphrase slots. libsodium's copy is
              the reference implementation plus SIMD kernels, from the same
              release as the AES-GCM file, so one provenance covers both.
    Tests:    tests/test_crypto.cpp — GCM spec test cases 13-16 and OpenSSL-
              computed tags for 113 B .. 64 KiB messages (tests/tools/gcm_reference.py);
              Argon2id: two of libsodium's own test vectors
              (test/default/pwhash_argon2id.c, tv cases 4 and 6).
